  "accuracyFacts": [
    "A backup is not proven usable by a successful BACKUP command or RESTORE VERIFYONLY alone. Restore the intended full/differential/log combinations to an isolated target, run consistency checks, and measure recovery against RPO/RTO.",
    "A differential database backup is based on the most recent conventional full backup, not a copy-only full backup. A copy-only full backup does not become the differential base. Log backups require an unbroken log chain under FULL or BULK_LOGGED recovery; changing to SIMPLE breaks the log chain for point-in-time recovery until a new data-backup sequence is established.",
    "In BULK_LOGGED recovery, a log backup can include minimally logged changes, and point-in-time recovery to a time inside that log backup is not available; recovery is to the end of that log backup. Confirm documented prerequisites before relying on a STOPAT target.",
    "RESTORE WITH RECOVERY makes the database available and ends the restore sequence. Use NORECOVERY while applying subsequent backups; STANDBY permits read-only access between restores but requires undo-file handling and is not the same as an availability replica.",
    "RESTORE VERIFYONLY checks that the backup set is complete and readable but does not verify the structure or semantics of the restored database. A real restore is required to validate recoverability.",
    "Automatic and planned no-data-loss availability-group failover require a synchronous-commit target that is synchronized and otherwise eligible; forced failover can lose data. Asynchronous commit acknowledges primary commits without waiting for secondary hardening, so RPO can be nonzero.",
    "An availability group improves availability/DR but is not a backup: deletions, bad writes, and corruption can propagate to replicas. Replication, CDC, and Change Tracking solve different data-movement or change-consumption needs and do not replace backups.",
    "SQL Server 2025 change event streaming is marked Preview in current release-status documentation. Its presence in an RTM build does not make the preview-supported feature generally available.",
    "Temporal period timestamps are generated by the database engine using the transaction begin time in UTC; temporal history is row-version history, not an audit identity, commit-order log, or backup. Retention can remove history, and all SYSTEM_TIME predicates have defined interval semantics.",
    "DBCC CHECKDB checks database consistency but is not a backup and repair can lose data. Prefer restoring a known-good backup to repair when possible; emergency repair is a last resort with explicit data-loss acceptance.",
    "Online index operation availability and restrictions depend on SQL Server version, edition, operation, and data/index type. Do not claim every index operation is online."
  ],
  "generationAllowed": true
